---
import CodeBlock from '../../components/CodeBlock.astro';
import Docs from '../../layouts/Docs.astro';
import { site } from '../../site';

const catalogManifest = `https://catalog.${site.domain}/manifest.json`;
const librivoxManifest = `https://catalog.${site.domain}/librivox/manifest.json`;
const internetArchiveManifest = `https://catalog.${site.domain}/internet-archive/manifest.json`;
const servicesUrl = `${site.repoUrl}/tree/main/services`;

const flow = `
GET https://addon.example.com/manifest.json
  → who you are, what you serve, which catalogs you have

GET https://addon.example.com/catalog/audiobook/new.json
  → { "metas": [ …titles and covers… ] }

GET https://addon.example.com/meta/audiobook/example%3Asecret-garden.json
  → { "meta": { …description, chapters… } }

GET https://addon.example.com/stream/audiobook/example%3Asecret-garden.json
  → { "streams": [ { "url": "https://media.example.com/…" } ] }
`;
---

<Docs
  title="Build an add-on"
  toc={[
    ['what', 'What an add-on does'],
    ['audio', 'Where audio goes'],
    ['start', 'Start here'],
    ['docs', 'Reference'],
    ['policy', 'Rules for add-ons'],
  ]}
  description={`How ${site.name} add-ons work: small HTTP services that answer JSON with catalogs, metadata and audio sources.`}
>
  <h1>Build an add-on for {site.name}</h1>
  <p class="lead">
    {site.name} is a player. Everything it can browse, search and stream online comes from add-ons:
    small, stateless HTTP services that answer a few <code>GET</code> requests with JSON.
  </p>

  <h2 id="what">What an add-on does</h2>
  <p>An add-on can answer any of three resources, for the types it declares:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>Catalogs</strong>: lists of titles to browse or search, such as “New releases” or
      “Poetry”.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Meta</strong>: the details of one item, such as the description, narrators, chapters
      or a podcast’s episodes.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Streams</strong>: where to play an item, as one audio URL or a list of parts.
    </li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    Types are <code>audiobook</code>, <code>podcast</code> and <code>episode</code>. An add-on
    describes itself in a manifest at <code>/manifest.json</code>; listeners add it by pasting that
    URL into the app. There is no account, no registration and no review.
  </p>
  <CodeBlock code={flow} label="A session with an add-on" />
  <p>
    Every request is a plain <code>GET</code> with everything it needs in the path, so responses
    cache well and an add-on runs anywhere that answers HTTP: Cloudflare Workers, Deno, Bun or Node.
  </p>

  <h2 id="audio">Audio goes straight to the listener</h2>
  <p>
    Stream URLs point at the source, and the app plays them from there. Audio never passes through{' '}
    {site.name}’s servers, and an add-on doesn’t have to serve it either: it only says where the
    files are. What a listener plays, and what they downloaded, stays on their device.
  </p>

  <h2 id="start">Start here</h2>
  <p>
    New to add-ons? <a href="/docs/guide/">Your first add-on</a> builds one from an empty folder: a
    catalog, then settings, then a source for your own files, then a deploy. Otherwise:
  </p>
  <ol>
    <li>
      <strong>Describe it.</strong> The <a href="/docs/manifest-builder/">manifest builder</a> turns
      a short form into a valid <code>manifest.json</code> and starter code for the SDK.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Pick a sample.</strong> The <a href="/docs/samples/">samples</a> are small, tested
      add-ons in TypeScript with Hono, in plain Node and in Python, plus one with hard-coded
      catalogs and no code beyond the SDK. Copy the one closest to your stack.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Fill in your data.</strong> The <a href="/docs/data-model/">data model samples</a>{' '}
      show every object the app reads, from a catalog row to a stream in parts.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Check it.</strong> <a href="/docs/checking/">Checking an add-on</a> runs it the way
      the app does and says what is wrong, on your machine and in your CI. It comes with SDK 0.6.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Put it online.</strong> <a href="/docs/hosting/">Hosting</a> covers Cloudflare
      Workers, Docker and any HTTPS host, and the CORS header every add-on needs.
    </li>
  </ol>

  <h2 id="docs">Reference</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <a href="/docs/protocol/">Protocol v1</a>: every route, object and field, with example
      responses and a <a href="/docs/protocol.schema.json">JSON Schema</a>.
    </li>
    <li>
      <a href="/docs/sdk/">SDK guide</a>: write an add-on in TypeScript with <code>@lelap/sdk</code>
      , run it on Cloudflare Workers, Deno or Bun, and add it to the app.
    </li>
    <li>
      <a href="/docs/mapping-files/">Mapping your files to works</a>: answer book pages from other
      add-ons with your own files, using the SDK’s matcher.
    </li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    For coding assistants such as Claude Code or Cursor: <a href="/llms.txt">llms.txt</a> lists
    these pages, and <a href="/llms-full.txt">llms-full.txt</a> holds all of them as Markdown in one
    file. The SDK package ships its own one-page guide as{' '}
    <code>node_modules/@lelap/sdk/llms.txt</code>.
  </p>
  <p>
    {site.name}’s own add-ons are built the same way: the Open Library catalog add-on ({' '}
    <code>{catalogManifest}</code>) brings Open Library and Podcast Index, and the LibriVox add-on ({' '}
    <code>{librivoxManifest}</code>) streams public-domain recordings. The Internet Archive add-on ({' '}
    <code>{internetArchiveManifest}</code>) brings old-time radio, lectures and readings from
    archive.org, only items in the public domain or under a Creative Commons licence; it isn’t
    installed by default, so add it by that address. Their source is in the{' '}
    <a href={servicesUrl}>repository</a>.
  </p>

  <h2 id="policy">Rules for add-ons</h2>
  <div class="note">
    <p>
      Only build add-ons for audio you have the rights to stream: public-domain works, your own
      recordings, feeds their publishers make public, or a library you run yourself.
    </p>
  </div>
  <ul>
    <li>
      {site.name} keeps no directory of third-party add-ons and doesn’t list, link to or recommend
      any. Share your add-on’s URL however you like; listeners decide what to install.
    </li>
    <li>
      You run your add-on and you answer for what it serves. Follow the terms of the services it
      connects to.
    </li>
    <li>
      Rights holders can reach us about our own sites and add-ons at{' '}
      <a href={`mailto:${site.copyrightEmail}`}>{site.copyrightEmail}</a>. See the{' '}
      <a href="/terms">terms</a>.
    </li>
  </ul>
</Docs>
